# Text operations.
#
# The seven primitives below, and six of them are here for a reason that
# is a fact about the language rather than an inconvenience:
#
#   textLength, textAt  there is no way to inspect a Text from inside the
#                       language, so nothing can take one apart
#   uppercase,
#   lowercase           Unicode case mapping is a table, not a rule
#   trim, newline       the lexer's string rule has no escapes, so no
#                       whitespace character can be written down at all —
#                       `trim` cannot name what it removes, and a line
#                       separator cannot be spelled as a literal
#
# The seventh is `split`, and it is the one primitive in the library whose
# reason is a measurement rather than an argument. `append item to list`
# made it writable and it was written; the note on its declaration below
# records what that cost and why it was put back. Every other operation
# that used to be `foreign` for returning a collection — `reverse`,
# `rest`, `keys`, `values` — is an ordinary ZDeceptron fold now and stays
# one, because none of them is what a document goes through.
#
# `newline` is the contrast worth naming: it is not a primitive because it
# builds something, but because the lexer cannot spell it. Construction
# stopped being a reason; the escape rule did not.
#
# Everything under them is ordinary ZDeceptron, walking by index and
# carrying its answer with it.
#
# ⚠️ TWO COST CLASSES, AND WHICH TO REACH FOR. `split` is the only
# primitive that walks a whole `Text` in one step, so it is the only route
# by which an operation survives a document.
#
#   * The **delimiter** family — `contains`, `before`, `after`,
#     `beforeLast`, `afterLast`, `withoutPrefix`, `withoutSuffix`,
#     `replace`, `lines` — is written over `split` and is linear in the
#     input. These are the operations a content site runs over a whole
#     file, and they are what makes a ten-thousand character document
#     something the library can hold.
#   * The **index** family — `slice`, `dropFirst`, `startsWith`,
#     `endsWith` — builds its result one code point at a time, because the
#     only way to make a `Text` the source did not write is `+`. Each
#     concatenation copies everything already built, so the cost is
#     quadratic in the number of characters *copied*. These are for
#     affixes, which are literals in practice: `"# "`, `".md"`, `"posts/"`.
#
# What the local bindings and the tail-call rewrite changed is depth, not
# time. Every fold below carries its answer as a parameter, so its last
# act is a call and the emitter turns it into a jump: stack depth is one
# frame regardless of input size, where it used to be one frame per
# character. That removes the failure mode — running the host out of
# stack — without moving either cost class.
# `crates/zdc-codegen/tests/depth.rs` holds the numbers.
#
# A `textSlice` primitive would flatten the index family's time as well;
# it is not taken, because §14F.2 asks for the operation in the language
# and the delimiter family already covers every document-scale use.

foreign textLength is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "length"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Whole

foreign textAt is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "at"
    takes value is Text, index is Whole
    gives Option of Text

foreign uppercase is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "uppercase"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

foreign lowercase is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "lowercase"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

foreign trim is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "trim"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

# The pieces of `value` between occurrences of `using`, in order.
#
# ⚠️ THE ONE PRIMITIVE THAT RETURNS A COLLECTION, AND IT IS A MEASUREMENT.
# `append item to list` made "returns a collection" stop being a reason to
# be `foreign`, and this was written in ZDeceptron on the strength of it:
# a fold matching `using` with `slice` at each index, gathering pieces
# with `append`, entirely ordinary. It was correct and it was kept out.
#
# Every operation below that a content site runs over a whole document
# goes through `split`, and as a fold it costs an interpreted loop
# iteration per character where the platform call costs one call. Measured
# on the ten-thousand character document in
# `zdc-codegen/tests/library.rs`, the delimiter family went from
# milliseconds to 416 seconds against a twenty-second budget. §14F.2 asks
# for the operation in the language where the language can express it; a
# library that cannot hold a document is not what it is asking for.
#
# `using is ""` is the characters of `value`, one piece each. JavaScript's
# own `split("")` divides a Text into UTF-16 units and hands back half a
# `🎉`; the emission special-cases it to divide the way `textAt` indexes,
# which is the same decision §5.4 makes everywhere else.
foreign split is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "split"
    takes value is Text, using is Text
    gives List of Text

# The line separator. Written as a bare name because §4.4 already says a
# callable declaring no parameters is one, which is the same route `clock`
# takes and needs no new syntax.
foreign newline is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "newline"
    gives Text

# --- the index family: affixes -------------------------------------------

# The characters of `value` from `start` up to but not including `stop`.
#
# O(k²) time in k = stop - start, per the cost note above, and one frame
# of stack whatever k is. For an affix, k is the length of a literal.
#
# The characters gathered so far travel as a parameter rather than as a
# pending `ch + …` around the recursive call, so the last thing this does
# is call itself and the emitter turns that into a jump (§17.4.10). It is
# the difference between a `slice` whose stack depth is the length of what
# it slices and one whose depth is one frame.
function slice with value, start, stop
    give sliceStep with value is value, index is start, stop is stop, taken is ""

function sliceStep with value, index, stop, taken
    if index < stop
        when textAt with value is value, index is index
            None
                give taken
            Some with ch
                give sliceStep with value is value, index is index + 1, stop is stop, taken is taken + ch
    give taken

# Whether `value` begins with `prefix`. O(p²) in the length of `prefix`.
function startsWith with value, prefix
    give (slice with value is value, start is 0, stop is textLength of prefix) is prefix

# Whether `value` ends with `suffix`. O(s²) in the length of `suffix`.
function endsWith with value, suffix
    give (dropFirst with value is value, count is (textLength of value) - (textLength of suffix)) is suffix

# `value` without its first `count` code points. O(k²) in what is kept.
function dropFirst with value, count
    give slice with value is value, start is count, stop is textLength of value

# --- the delimiter family: whole documents -------------------------------

# The target of `value contains needle` when `value` is a Text. O(n + m).
#
# A `Text` splits into more than one piece exactly when the separator
# occurs in it, so containment is one primitive call and a length.
#
# The character walk §17.4.9 shows instead — compare a `slice` at every
# index — is the alternative, and the tail-call rewrite fixed half of what
# was wrong with it: written as a plain recursion its stack depth was O(n)
# and a ten-thousand character document ran the host out of stack, and
# written in tail position its depth is one frame. What the rewrite does
# not change is the time, which stays O(n · m²) against this line's
# O(n + m). So the walk is now merely slow rather than fatal, and this is
# still the spelling to keep.
function textContains with value, needle
    if needle is ""
        give yes
    give (listLength of (split with value is value, using is needle)) > 1

# Where `needle` first occurs in `value`, counted in code points, and
# `None` when it does not occur. O(n + m).
#
# An `Option of Whole` rather than a sentinel `0 - 1`: §5.4 makes an
# out-of-range answer a variant everywhere else in the language, and a
# position is exactly the place a sentinel would be indexed with.
function indexOf with value, needle
    if needle is ""
        give Some with value is 0
    if value contains needle
        give Some with value is (textLength of (before with value is value, delimiter is needle))
    give None

# The text before the first `delimiter`, or the whole of `value` when the
# delimiter does not occur. O(n).
function before with value, delimiter
    if delimiter is ""
        give ""
    give valueOr with maybe is (first of (split with value is value, using is delimiter)), fallback is value

# The text after the first `delimiter`, or "" when it does not occur.
#
# O(n · p) in p, the number of pieces `split` produces: the pieces after
# the first are put back together by `joinFrom`, which walks them in one
# frame rather than one per piece.
#
# `start is 1` as well as `index is 1`, so the piece at 1 is taken without
# a separator in front of it and the delimiter reappears only between the
# pieces it originally separated.
function after with value, delimiter
    if delimiter is ""
        give value
    give joinFrom with parts is (split with value is value, using is delimiter), using is delimiter, start is 1, index is 1, joined is ""

# The text before the last `delimiter`, or "" when it does not occur.
# O(n · p), for the same reason as `after`.
function beforeLast with value, delimiter
    if delimiter is ""
        give ""
    give joinAllButLast with parts is (split with value is value, using is delimiter), using is delimiter

# The text after the last `delimiter`, or the whole of `value` when it
# does not occur. O(n).
function afterLast with value, delimiter
    if delimiter is ""
        give value
    give valueOr with maybe is (last of (split with value is value, using is delimiter)), fallback is value

# `value` with `prefix` removed if it is there, unchanged if it is not.
# O(n · p + q²) in the length q of the prefix.
function withoutPrefix with value, prefix
    if prefix is ""
        give value
    if startsWith with value is value, prefix is prefix
        give after with value is value, delimiter is prefix
    give value

# `value` with `suffix` removed if it is there, unchanged if it is not.
# O(n · p + s²) in the length s of the suffix.
function withoutSuffix with value, suffix
    if suffix is ""
        give value
    if endsWith with value is value, suffix is suffix
        give beforeLast with value is value, delimiter is suffix
    give value

# Every occurrence of `old` replaced by `new`. O(n · p): split, then join.
#
# `old` and `new` rather than `from` and `to` because both of those are
# reserved words (§14G.7.7's table is the whole budget, and this library
# spends none of it).
function replace with value, old, new
    if old is ""
        give value
    give join with parts is (split with value is value, using is old), using is new

# A document as its lines. O(n).
function lines of value
    give split with value is value, using is newline

# Lines back as a document. O(n · p), which is `join`'s cost.
function unlines of parts
    give join with parts is parts, using is newline

function isBlank of value
    give (trim of value) is ""
